meta {
  name: Image with GET
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{imagesUrl}}/as/web-large/DP251139.jpg
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Origin: http://localhost:8103
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("a GET carries no Access-Control-Allow-Origin header", function () {
    expect(res.getHeader("access-control-allow-origin")).to.equal(undefined);
  });

  test("it is a JPEG", function () {
    expect(res.getHeader("content-type")).to.equal("image/jpeg");
  });
}

docs {
  The picture of object 45734, as its `primaryImageSmall` gives it.

  The response has no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, even with an
  `Origin` sent. Flutter web draws to a canvas and has to fetch an
  image's bytes to draw it, which the browser refuses for another origin
  without that header. So the app shows every picture in a real `<img>`
  element instead (`ArtworkImage`).

  Compare with **Image with HEAD**.
}
